  "worked_solution": "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{\u2032})+g({y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{\u2032}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{\u2032})={y}^{\u2032},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{\u2032})+g({y}^{\u2032})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{\u2032})=({y}^{\u2032})^{3},\\,g({y}^{\u2032})=({y}^{\u2032})^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{\u2032}}-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{\u2032}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{\u2032})+g({y}^{\u2032}).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{\u2032}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{\u2032})+g({y}^{\u2032}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
